ly distinct binaries with separate controller sets (manager vs. cloudmanager); no shared artifacts being built. Merge proximity is a coordination risk, not a dependency. Keeping them separate also honors the open release-phasing question for cloudmanager — either epic can be scheduled independently. |\n| E001 → E003 | 11 | `docs-authoring` epic is blocked by all Implementation epics; the guideline documents the convention as implemented, including before/after examples drawn from the converted code. |\n| E002 → E003 | 11 | Same rule — the guideline covers the convention across both binaries. |\n\n## Component Mapping\n\nThe strategy names a single affected component, **rhods-operator**. That name does not appear verbatim in\n`.context/rhai-components.txt`; the closest canonical Jira component is **OpenShift AI Productization**, which\ncovers the rhods-operator (red-hat-data-services/rhods-operator, midstream opendatahub-operator) work owned by\nthe ODH Operator team. All three epics use `OpenShift AI Productization`; submit.py would silently strip a\nnon-canonical value such as \"rhods-operator\".\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic |\n|-----|----------|------|\n| Convert message-embedded resource identity in reconciliation error logs to structured `name`/`namespace` KV pairs | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 |\n| Migrate DSCI controller from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase convention | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 |\n| Service controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 |\n| All 15 action-file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` across 4 tiers | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 |\n| Cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 |\n| Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 module handlers) | P0 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 |\n| DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 |\n| Document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external adoption | P1 | RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 |\n\nFull coverage: all 8 HLRs map to at least one epic. The P1 HLR (DSC/component controller `resourceKind`) is\nbundled into E001 rather than split: it touches the same log call sites, files, and test surfaces as the P0\nconversion work — it is a queryability enhancement incidental to the same implementation, not a deferrable\nfeature on a separate surface.\n\n## Epic Boundary Rationale\n\n- **E001 vs. E002**: same component and team, but manager and cloudmanager are architecturally distinct\n  sub-systems (two binaries, disjoint controller sets, different deployment targets — OpenShift vs. EKS/AKS/\n  CoreWeave). Split along the sub-system boundary per Rule 2; the strategy's open question on cloudmanager\n  release phasing makes independent planning valuable.\n- **E001 vs. E003 / E002 vs. E003**: code implementation vs. substantive content authoring are different\n  work-product types with different review criteria (Rule 2). The guideline is a new doc topic with authored\n  content (convention spec, rationale, before/after examples, migration guide), not an incidental touch-up.\n- **CI lint rule** (flagging `log.Error` calls with embedded identity or missing required fields) is folded\n  into E001's scope: it is repo-wide CI configuration that enforces the conversion E001 performs — same\n  change, same review cycle, no independent user value.\n- **COO (Loki/Tempo Operator) adoption**: no epic. Per platform policy overlay 0008, RHOAI does not install,\n  manage, or control external operators; adoption is voluntary and at the COO team's discretion. The\n  deliverable RHOAI controls is publishing the convention and filing tracking issues, which is E003 scope.\n\n## Investigation Assessment\n\nNo Investigation epics. Each open question was evaluated against \"does the answer change which downstream\nepics exist or what they do?\":\n\n- *Additional fields beyond name/namespace/kind* (PM/Observability): changes the convention doc's content at\n  most; the required field set for the code work is fixed by the HLRs and Staff Engineer input. Captured as an\n  open question on E003.\n- *Which alerts rely on message-string patterns* (SRE/Support): resolved during the regression-verification\n  step the strategy already prescribes; captured as an acceptance criterion on E001/E002, not an epic.\n- *Cloudmanager release phasing* (PM): a scheduling decision — the work exists either way. Handled by keeping\n  E002 edge-free (independently schedulable); noted in Health Warnings.\n- *Actual percentage of embedded-identity log calls*: the audit is the first implementation step of E001; the\n  conversion scope (\"all reconciliation error log call sites\") is unchanged by the number.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Release phasing (non-blocking)**: cloudmanager (E002) may follow in a subsequent release per the open\n  question owned by PM/ODH Operator team. The DAG intentionally leaves E002 parallel so either answer is\n  supported; if deferred, E003's blocker set still includes it.\n- **Log consumer regression risk**: text-based alert rules that match current message strings may break.\n  Mitigation (dev-cluster verification with production log consumer stack, SRE/Support coordination) is an\n  acceptance criterion on E001; the DSCI field-name migration should land in a single release to avoid\n  long-term query inconsistency.\n- **Process gates pending**: Architecture Review and other prerequisites are marked \"Pending human review\" in\n  the strategy — standard sign-off, not structural unknowns.\n- **Priority note**: the strategy's proposed acceptance criteria state they \"require PM/Engineering\n  validation\"; ACs below are derived from the strategy as written.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable. The only external dependency (COO team adoption) is voluntary and outside RHOAI control by\npolicy; no 