{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-adb08a6a331a7a54","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n3\ttitle: Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource\n4\t  context\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tdependencies: []\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 0\n17\t  external_dependency: 1\n18\t  human_process_gates: -1\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 5\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Standardize manager binary reconciliation error logs to structured resource context\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tConvert all reconciliation error log calls in the rhods-operator manager binary that embed resource name or namespace in unstructured message strings into structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), add `resourceKind` as a structured field across all controller tiers and action files, and migrate the DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller from its non-standard field names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to the standard camelCase convention. This makes reconciliation error logs queryable by resource name, namespace, and kind across all controllers in the manager binary, and adds a CI guard so the convention holds for future code.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Audit all `log.Error()` call sites in reconciliation error paths across the manager binary's five controller tiers: the DataScienceCluster (DSC) controller, the 8 component controllers (DataSciencePipelines, Kueue, ModelRegistry, Ray, SparkOperator, Trainer, TrainingOperator, TrustyAI), the module controller with its 8 module handlers (Dashboard, AIGateway, KServe, OGX, Workbenches, MLflowOperator, FeastOperator, MCPLifecycleOperator), the DSCInitialization (DSCI) controller, and the 5 service controllers (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup). Track audit coverage in a checklist; confirm the actual share of calls embedding resource identity (SME estimate: 75%).\n39\t- Convert every audited call site that embeds resource name or namespace in the message string to structured key-value pairs using the fields `name` and `namespace`, removing the redundant identity from the message text.\n40\t- Migrate DSCI controller structured field names from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to `name`/`namespace`; no references to the old field names may remain.\n41\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to reconciliation error logs in: service controllers, the module controller and its module handlers, all action files, and the DSC and 8 component controllers (for the latter, kind is implied by the controller name — this is a queryability enhancement).\n42\t- Cover all 15 action files across 4 tiers: 8 component action files (`components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go`), 3 service action files (`services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}/*_controller_actions.go`), 1 DSC action file (`datasciencecluster/datasciencecluster_controller_actions.go`), 3 module action files (`modules/modules_controller_actions*.go`).\n43\t- Handle cluster-scoped resources (e.g., DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) correctly: emit `name` and `resourceKind`, with `namespace` omitted or set to empty string — never a spurious namespace value.\n44\t- Add a repo-wide CI lint rule or static analysis check that flags any `log.Error` call embedding name/namespace in the message text or missing the required structured fields.\n45\t- Add unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via a test logger for the converted call sites.\n46\t- Backwards-compatibility verification per the strategy NFR: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error, confirming structured fields appear and that existing log consumers (Loki, CloudWatch) ingest both formats without configuration changes; coordinate with SRE/Support to identify and update text-based alert rules that rely on current message-string patterns.\n47\t- Rollback/release-notes handling for the DSCI field-name migration: document the change with before/after query examples so operators using the old field names can update saved queries (full migration guide authoring belongs to the convention documentation effort; this epic supplies the release-notes entry).\n48\t\n49\t## Acceptance Criteria\n50\t\n51\t- Given any manager-binary reconciliation error log call that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, the updated call uses structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) instead — measured by the CI lint rule / static analysis passing with zero flagged `log.Error` calls across the manager binary.\n52\t- Given the DSCI controller's reconciliation error logs, all structured field names use the camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) — measured by unit tests asserting the standard field names and a codebase grep confirming no references to `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` remain.\n53\t- Given a service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) reconciliation error, the log entry includes `resourceKind` — measured by unit tests parsing test logger output and asserting `resourceKind` is present with the correct value for each of the 5 service controllers.\n54\t- Given an action file error log in any of the 4 tiers, the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` — measured by unit tests asserting all three fields are present with correct values across all 15 action files.\n55\t- Given a module controller error log for any of the 8 module handlers, the entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` — measured by unit tests over test logger output.\n56\t- Given a DSC or component controller reconciliation error, the entry includes `resourceKind` — measured by unit tests across the DSC controller and all 8 component controllers.\n57\t- Given a cluster-scoped resource (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization) reconciliation error, the entry includes `name` and `resourceKind` with `namespace` omitted or empty — measured by unit tests confirming no spurious namespace value.\n58\t- Given a sample reconciliation error before and after the change, the before/after log output comparison shows the structured fields present, and the production log consumer stack ingests both formats without configuration changes — measured by a dev-cluster test-build verification with the consumer stack configured and existing alert queries confirmed to still match (after SRE/Support-coordinated updates where needed).\n59\t- Given the DSCI field-name migration, a rollback path exists (revert of the migration commit) and the release-notes entry with before/after query examples is drafted — measured by review of the release-notes draft alongside the merge.\n60\t\n61\t## HLR Traceability\n62\t\n63\t- [P0] All rhods-operator reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings are converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`) — manager binary portion\n64\t- [P0] DSCI controller migrated from non-standard key names (`Request.Name`, `DSCInitialization`) to camelCase convention (`name`, `namespace`)\n65\t- [P0] All service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` as a structured field\n66\t- [P0] All action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields across all 4 tiers (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module action files)\n67\t- [P0] Module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields (8 module handlers)\n68\t- [P1] DSC and component controller reconciliation error logs include `resourceKind` for cross-controller queryability\n69\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f855ef2a480931a","agent_id":"a96558e77e1b7cd9e","session_id":"b85c52ef-139b-4b48-a119-7718fd584270","uuid":"1ba45ac0-9fda-4c82-ab27-24c1a56bea77","timestamp":"2026-10-22T03:17:53.187Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-adb08a6a331a7a54","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
